import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.D26l3Sq6.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Memory & ownership","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"implementation/ownership.md","filePath":"implementation/ownership.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"implementation/ownership.md"};function n(o,e,l,d,h,c){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="Memory-and-ownership" tabindex="-1">Memory &amp; ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#Memory-and-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Memory &amp; ownership {#Memory-and-ownership}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>VTK is reference-counted; Julia is garbage-collected. The bridge between them is the subtlest part of VTK.jl. <strong>Object lifetime and data lifetime are separate questions:</strong> an owning handle can keep a VTK object alive while that object&#39;s contents continue to alias, and change with, a parent or pipeline.</p><h2 id="Lifetime-rules-by-API-kind" tabindex="-1">Lifetime rules by API kind <a class="header-anchor" href="#Lifetime-rules-by-API-kind" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lifetime rules by API kind {#Lifetime-rules-by-API-kind}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>Factories own.</strong> <code>UnstructuredGrid()</code>, <code>Points()</code>, <code>read_vtu(...)</code>, and similar constructors return a CxxWrap <code>vtkSmartPointer{T}</code> holding a strong VTK reference. Julia&#39;s finalizer queues the wrapper for deferred destruction; a safe drain releases that reference, and VTK destroys the object when its reference count reaches zero.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>take_ref</code> getters own a VTK reference; they are not merely borrowed.</strong> <code>point_data(grid)</code>, <code>points(grid)</code>, <code>field_array_raw(...)</code>, and other getters implemented with <code>take_ref</code> call <code>Register</code> before returning a smart pointer. The resulting Julia handle therefore keeps the referenced object alive independently of the parent handle. It still denotes the <em>same</em> object as the parent uses, so mutations are shared while both refer to it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Raw <code>CxxPtr</code> values are truly borrowed.</strong> Low-level conversions such as <code>_cp(...)</code> do not call <code>Register</code> and do not extend lifetime. Keep the owning smart pointer (and, where relevant, its parent/container) alive for every raw-pointer call. Do not store a raw pointer beyond the owner&#39;s lifetime.</p></li><li><p><strong>Pipeline outputs own the output object&#39;s lifetime, not a snapshot of its contents.</strong> <code>GetOutput</code> uses <code>take_ref</code>, so the returned data object can outlive the algorithm handle. However, a reader or filter can reuse and mutate that same object on a later <code>Update</code>. Retaining the handle prevents use-after-free; it does not prevent the next update from changing its data.</p></li><li><p><strong>Shallow copies share storage.</strong> A VTK <code>ShallowCopy</code> creates another data object but shares underlying arrays with the source. Mutating shared array contents remains visible through both objects.</p></li><li><p><strong>Deep copies are detached.</strong> <code>DeepCopy</code>/<code>DeepCopyDataObject</code> duplicate the data arrays as well as the containing object, so later source or pipeline updates do not alter the copy. <code>Array(a::VTKDataArray)</code> likewise copies the current values into independent Julia storage.</p></li></ul><p>GC-stress tests with thousands of allocations and surviving objects exercise the smart-pointer ownership path.</p><h2 id="Temporal-results" tabindex="-1">Temporal results <a class="header-anchor" href="#Temporal-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Temporal results {#Temporal-results}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>read_timestep</code>, <code>read_at</code>, and <code>eachstep</code> default to <code>copy=true</code>, returning a deep copy that remains unchanged by later reader updates. Passing <code>copy=false</code> explicitly selects the pipeline-output rule above: the handle owns a reference to the output object, but its contents are borrowed from the mutable pipeline and must be consumed or copied before the next update.</p><p>The one-shot <code>read_ensight(path)</code> and <code>read_cgns(path)</code> helpers intentionally avoid that extra copy. Their temporary reader is never advanced again, and the returned output handle owns the reference needed to keep the object alive.</p><p><code>timesteps(reader)</code> returns a copy of the time vector. Mutating it neither changes reader metadata nor affects subsequent timestep selection.</p><h2 id="Views-own-through-the-wrapper" tabindex="-1">Views own through the wrapper <a class="header-anchor" href="#Views-own-through-the-wrapper" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Views own through the wrapper {#Views-own-through-the-wrapper}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>data(x)</code> returns a <code>VTKDataArray</code> that <em>holds</em> the smart pointer as a field; <code>raw_data(x)</code> returns a flat view derived from that wrapper. The derivation chain — <code>ReshapedArray → VTKDataArray → SmartPointer</code> — keeps the C++ object alive <strong>structurally</strong>, with no finalizers on views:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">da </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> VTK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">data_array</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(Float64, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">VTK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">resize!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(da, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">4</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">v </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> VTK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">raw_data</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(da</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">obj)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">da </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> nothing</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; GC</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">gc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># the original handle is gone…</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">v[</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]                    </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># …and the view still owns a share of the object</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is the &quot;wrapper-owns-everything&quot; design. After the <em>last</em> wrapper/view chain is collected, its finalizer adds the native wrapper to the deferred deletion queue; the VTK reference is released at the next safe drain. Keeping an object alive does <strong>not</strong> keep an old data buffer valid after reallocation; see below.</p><h2 id="Cached-view-synchronization-and-resize-semantics" tabindex="-1">Cached-view synchronization and resize semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#Cached-view-synchronization-and-resize-semantics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cached-view synchronization and resize semantics {#Cached-view-synchronization-and-resize-semantics}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>VTKDataArray</code> caches its zero-copy <code>Matrix</code>, the array&#39;s VTK modification time (<code>GetMTime</code>), its dimensions, and its data-buffer address. Before array operations it compares that signature with the current array. VTK operations such as <code>SetNumberOfTuples</code> do not necessarily advance <code>GetMTime</code>, so the shape and address checks are essential. If another wrapper, <code>a.obj</code>, or a raw VTK call has structurally changed the array, the wrapper rebuilds its view before proceeding. <code>refresh!(a)</code> forces the same rebuild explicitly. This makes independently wrapped aliases coherent without making scalar indexing type-unstable.</p><p><code>resize!</code> calls VTK&#39;s <code>SetNumberOfTuples</code>, validates that the new tuple count is nonnegative, and refreshes immediately. There are two important limits:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Old independently obtained views still dangle after reallocation.</strong> Synchronization updates a <code>VTKDataArray</code>; it cannot retrofit a previously extracted matrix, raw buffer, or derived view whose pointer or dimensions belong to the old allocation. Discard and recreate such views after any operation that might reallocate. Never test or &quot;repair&quot; a dangling buffer by reading it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Growth is uninitialized.</strong> VTK may expose allocator leftovers in newly grown tuples. Initialize the grown region before reading it or performing whole-array reductions.</p></li></ol><p>These signature checks are not a thread-safety mechanism; concurrent structural mutation and Julia array access remain unsafe.</p><h2 id="Multiblock-containers" tabindex="-1">Multiblock containers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Multiblock-containers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multiblock containers {#Multiblock-containers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>GetBlockByIndex</code>, and therefore <code>block(mb, i)</code>, uses <code>take_ref</code>. The returned block owns a VTK reference and <strong>can outlive the parent multiblock handle or container</strong>. It is not a detached copy: while the block remains in <code>mb</code>, parent and child handles refer to the same object, so mutation through either is visible through the other. Replacing or removing the block changes the container&#39;s reference, not the already returned handle&#39;s object.</p><p><code>set_block!(mb, i, child)</code> uses VTK&#39;s <code>SetBlock</code>, which retains <code>child</code> in the container. The caller does <strong>not</strong> need to keep its original child handle alive afterward. Retention again means shared ownership, not copying: mutating the child through any surviving handle mutates the block stored in the parent.</p><h2 id="GC-finalizer-threads-and-deferred-destruction" tabindex="-1">GC finalizer threads and deferred destruction <a class="header-anchor" href="#GC-finalizer-threads-and-deferred-destruction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GC finalizer threads and deferred destruction {#GC-finalizer-threads-and-deferred-destruction}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>VTK smart-pointer wrappers require special treatment. CxxWrap&#39;s default finalizer deletes the C++ smart pointer immediately; that can call <code>UnRegister</code> and <code>vtkGarbageCollector::Collect</code> on a GC finalizer thread while another thread executes a filter. This caused reproducible heap corruption in the threaded test suite.</p><p>The wrapper now specializes CxxWrap&#39;s <code>Finalizer&lt;vtkSmartPointer&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> to <strong>enqueue</strong> the native wrapper without entering VTK. Queue insertion uses a dedicated mutex, so finalizers on multiple GC threads are safe.</p><p><code>drain_vtk_deletions()</code> performs the actual C++ deletions. With one Julia thread, high-level operations and common factories drain automatically at call boundaries; no other Julia thread can be inside VTK at those points. With multiple Julia threads, automatic draining is disabled because factories and raw calls can be active independently of scoped operations. At a verified quiescent point, after all VTK tasks/native worker threads have finished, call:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">VTK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">drain_vtk_deletions</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(force </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Without <code>force=true</code>, the multithreaded call throws rather than risking concurrent destruction. Raw-only workloads use the same explicit function for reclamation. Queued wrappers use memory until a drain, so applications running large multithreaded batches should establish regular quiescent points. Process exit may reclaim any remaining queue without entering VTK again.</p><p>Views themselves still have no owner-capturing finalizers; structural ownership and deferred native destruction are complementary. Do not attach custom finalizers that mutate or delete VTK objects.</p><h2 id="Practical-checklist" tabindex="-1">Practical checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#Practical-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Practical checklist {#Practical-checklist}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>Keep owning wrappers alive around every raw <code>CxxPtr</code> call.</p></li><li><p>Treat getters and multiblock children as owning aliases, not borrowed raw pointers and not detached copies.</p></li><li><p>Treat retained pipeline outputs as mutable until explicitly deep-copied.</p></li><li><p>Discard all previously derived buffers/views after possible reallocation.</p></li><li><p>Initialize freshly grown storage before reading or reducing it.</p></li><li><p>Establish quiescent <code>drain_vtk_deletions(force=true)</code> points after multithreaded/raw batches.</p></li><li><p>Do not attach custom finalizers that mutate or delete VTK objects or views.</p></li></ul>`,30)])])}const k=a(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,k as default};
